<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1535">1535</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Bihzad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād, or Bihzad, was a Persian painter of the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, the most celebrated master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat-school">Herat school</a>. He worked under Timurid patronage at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a>. His style shaped later Safavid and Mughal painting, a key part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>'s artistic legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamal-ud-din-behzad.jpg" alt="Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād" width="234" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Picture_of_the_master_Behzad_unknow_artist.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Behzād was born and lived most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in present-day western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, the cultural and economic capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>. The year of his birth is unknown. According to the author Qadi Ahmad he was orphaned young and raised by the painter and calligrapher Mirak Naqqash, a director of the Timurid royal library. He was also a protégé of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, poet and humanist Mir Ali-Shir Nava'i at the court of Sultan Husayn Bayqara, who ruled from 1469 to 1506.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> made in the sultan's library in the 1480s show Behzād's participation. In 1486, by decree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-bayqara">Husayn Bayqara</a>, he was appointed head of the royal ateliers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in succession to Mirak Naqqash, and under his leadership the Herat academy reached its highest point. After the sultan's death in 1506, Herat was taken by troops of the Bukhara Khanate under Mohammed Shaybani Khan. A fable says that Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> hid him in a cave during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a> in 1514.</p><p>In 1522 Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> employed him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, capital of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, as director of the royal atelier, calling him a miracle of the century in the decree. Ismail died in 1524, and Behzād's later work is usually associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp I</a>, in whose workshop he served until his death. His tomb lies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> beneath Kôh-i Mukhtâr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Behzād is better understood as the director of a workshop producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript-illumination">manuscript illuminations</a> in a style he conceived than as a lone painter. His compositions use geometric architecture and landscape, with unpatterned areas around which the figures are placed, and he used dark and light contrast more emphatically than other medieval miniaturists. His figures carry realistic gestures and expressions. His best-known works are The Seduction of Yusuf, from Sa'di's Bustan of 1488, and the paintings of the British Library's Nizami manuscript of 1494–95. Most works commonly attributed to him date from 1488 to 1495, and the attribution of individual paintings is often problematic. His fame led many to copy him, and rulers such as the Mughal emperors paid large sums for his paintings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Timurid painting, above all the Herat school associated with <b>Kamal al-Din Bihzad</b>, established the aesthetic tradition that the Safavid and Mughal courts developed into one of the most refined artistic traditions in Islamic history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1505" aria-label="Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6132 920 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6582 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1505">What was here in 1505? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
